#{[17592186512889 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512889 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112756" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512889 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512889 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $f(x)=a{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+bx+1$ has a factor of $(x-1)$ and has a remainder of $4$ when divided by $(x+2).$ Find the value of $a-b.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512889 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5db961df-fac4-59ee-a59e-6595583c0931" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512889 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x)$, it must divide into $f(x)$ with no remainder. In other words, when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x-1),$ the remainder is $0.$ It follows from the remainder theorem that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =f(1) \\\\  & =a(1)^{3}-2(1)^{2}+b(1)+1 \\\\  & =a-2+b+1 \\\\  & =a+b-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, since we're told that the remainder is $4$ when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+2),$ it follows from the remainder theorem that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =f(-2) \\\\  & =a(-2)^{3}-2(-2)^{2}+b(-2)+1 \\\\  & =-8a-8-2b+1 \\\\  & =-8a-2b-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0=a+b-1 \\\\ 4=-8a-2b-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying each equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}a+b=1 \\\\ 8a+2b=-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $b,$ we multiply the first equation by $-2,$ and we add the resulting equation to the second equation. So we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -2a-2b=-2 \\\\ +\\,\\,\\, & \\,8a+2b=-11 \\\\  & \\,6a=-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get $a=-\\frac{13}{6}.$ Finally, we substitute it into $a+b=1$ to find $b=\\frac{19}{6}.$ \n\nTherefore, our final solution is $a-b=-\\frac{16}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512891 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512891 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66857" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512891 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512891 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt[4]{e})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512891 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5f3a9a12-37bd-5534-bce9-24153ef1f5e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512891 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rewrite $\\sqrt[4]{e}$ as a power of $e$ (the base of the natural logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4]{e}={e}^{1/4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt[4]{e})=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{1/4})\n$$\n\nThe natural logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{1/4})=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{8}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512892 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512892 #edb/ref 1069 "q-3414" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512892 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512892 #edb/ref 1071 "Caden sold half of his comic books and then bought sixteen more. If he now has $36$, how many comic books did he have at the start?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512892 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "671acf01-53e4-5ebe-ba29-cc75ccac1d67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512892 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x=$ the number of comic books Caden had at the start.\n\nWe note that:\n\n- Half the number of comic books corresponds to $\\frac{1}{2}x.$\n\n- The number of comic books remaining after selling half of them corresponds to $x-\\frac{1}{2}x.$\n\n- The number of comic books remaining after selling half and buying sixteen more corresponds to $x-\\frac{1}{2}x+16.$\n\nThe final number of comic books is $36,$ so we have the equation\n\n$$\nx-\\frac{1}{2}x+16=36.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{1}{2}x+16 & =36 \\\\ (1-\\frac{1}{2})x+16 & =36 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+16 & =36 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+16-16 & =36-16 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =20 \\\\ 2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x & =2\\cdot 20 \\\\ x & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, Caden had $40$ comic books at the start." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512898 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512898 #edb/ref 1069 "q-118876" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512898 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512898 #edb/ref 1071 "A company rents ten identical storage units in the form of a rectangular prism. Each unit is $5\\,\\text{yd}$ long, $3\\,\\text{yd}$ wide, and $3\\text{yd}$ high. If it costs $$2$ per cubic yard per day to rent a storage unit, how much does it cost the company each day to rent the storage units?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512898 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "751e0aa2-a931-559b-9a2f-5250360d1346" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512898 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the volume of one storage unit.\n\nWe can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=5,\\,w=3,\\,h=3\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=5\\cdot 3\\cdot 3=45\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe total volume of the ten units is\n\n$$\n45\\cdot 10=450\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to rent the storage units, each day the company must pay\n\n$$\n450\\cdot $2=$900.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512902 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512902 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34313" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512902 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512902 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/23/23f0dba237502bc7261fd8f28327beefd5872bb4b0f503930c488b2857fcef24.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(-\\sqrt{3},-1),$ calculate the angle $\\theta .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512902 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e50efe9-568f-54f5-835e-03a6e09d3518" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512902 #edb/ref 1074 "We can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis. Let us call ${\\theta }^{′}$ the acute angle that is formed with the $x$-axis such that ${\\theta }^{′}+{180}^{∘}=\\theta $\n\n![](images/d1/d14f62179f831ebdc177eac7bd2899679864305d4104d4b61f304eb4fa9e133d.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n{\\theta }^{′}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}={30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using $\\theta ={\\theta }^{′}+{180}^{∘},$ we get $\\theta ={30}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}={210}^{∘}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512908 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512908 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112414" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512908 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512908 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the solution $(a,b)$ to the following system of equations by eliminating the variable $a:$ \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5a+4b=7 \\\\ -2a+6b=20\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512908 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8b8bf0b2-6f55-5107-9e2d-cc11b6142890" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512908 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the $a$-coefficient in the first equation is $5,$ while in the second equation it is $-2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $2$ and the second equation by $5,$ then the $a$-coefficient in the first and second equations will become $10$ and $-10,$ and these will cancel when we add the equations.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,5a+4b=7\\,)\\,\\times (2) \\\\ (\\,-2a+6b=20\\,)\\,\\times (5)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}10a+8b=14 \\\\ -10a+30b=100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $a$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{10a}+8b & =14 \\\\ -\\cancel{10a}+30b & =100 \\\\ (8b+30b) & =(14+100) \\\\ 38b & =114 \\\\ b & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $b=3.$ However, we still need to find $a.$ To do this, we substitute $b=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a+4b & =7 \\\\ 5a+4(3) & =7 \\\\ 5a+12 & =7 \\\\ 5a & =-5 \\\\ a & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-1$ and $b=3,$ which can be written as $(-1,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512917 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512917 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66526" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512917 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512917 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512917 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a5359436-369e-5935-88c6-73a8053a9364" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512917 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}$ as a power of $e$ (the base of the natural logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{1/2}}={e}^{-1/2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-1/2})\n$$\n\nThe natural logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-1/2})=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512938 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512938 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85920" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512938 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512938 #edb/ref 1071 "A team of $5$ players is to be chosen at random from $7$ boys and $5$ girls.  What is the probability that $2$ boys and $3$ girls will be chosen? Round your answer to the nearest percent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512938 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7f9805c-ce96-515d-891a-332594997042" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512938 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A$ represent the event described in the question. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{n}{N},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the total number of ways to choose $5$ people out of a total of $12,$ and $n$ is the number of picks where $2$ are boys and $3$ are girls.\n\nSince the order in which the players are chosen does not matter, we use combinations.\n\nFirst, we compute $N.$ The number of ways to pick $5$ people from a total of $12$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N={}_{12}{C}_{5} & =\\frac{12!}{5!\\cdot 7!}=792.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $n.$ The computation of $n$ can be divided into two steps: \n\n- the number of ways to choose $2$ (out of $7$) boys is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{7}{C}_{2}=\\frac{7!}{2!\\cdot 5!}=21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the number of ways to choose $3$ (out of $5$) girls is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{5}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{5!}{3!\\cdot 2!}=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the results from the above steps, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n={}_{7}{C}_{2}\\cdot {}_{5}{C}_{3}=21\\cdot 10=210.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{210}{792}≈0.27=27%.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513032 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513056 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513032 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513080 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513032 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513088 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513032 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513032 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ef/efc2a0fcf4999a4ab32cdd5957a87ec7bf782cdb1ecac743551fd0509b983f6b.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nI. $x\\in (-2,0]$\n\nII. $x\\in [0,4]$\n\nIII. $x\\in [-1,4]$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513032 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0a972469-92c6-5025-b8f5-75835bec6279" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513032 #edb/ref 1074 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- On interval I, $x\\in (-2,0]$, the function is invertible. Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the restricted graph at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- On interval II, $x\\in [0,4]$, the function is invertible. Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the restricted graph at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- On interval III, $x\\in [-1,4]$, the function is **not** invertible. Some horizontal lines will intersect the restricted graph more than once.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513034 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513071 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513034 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513074 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513034 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513086 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513034 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513034 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/62/62233fc5bc77f34837790174b42f6bf3652bfbc55fbd031b3881d072f0cd2475.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nI. $x\\in (-2,0]$\n\nII. $x\\in [0,3]$\n\nIII. $x\\in [-1,3]$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513034 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0ff7a1fc-3587-5ea1-8ffc-8f53b8613d93" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513034 #edb/ref 1074 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- On interval I, $x\\in (-2,0]$, the function is invertible. Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the restricted graph at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- On interval II, $x\\in [0,3]$, the function is invertible. Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the restricted graph at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- On interval III, $x\\in [-1,3]$, the function is **not** invertible. Some horizontal lines will intersect the restricted graph more than once.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513043 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513037 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513043 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513039 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513043 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513048 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513043 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513043 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e6/e6fe5387a8fcd1ac6dc679ebff16ed15f2ecdf6e84b035e209aa869970357dfd.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nI. $x\\in (-2,-1]\\cup (2,4]$\n\nII. $x\\in [0,2]$\n\nIII. $x\\in [-2,2]$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513043 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2bc33971-dd15-55a7-b231-7bebd785a762" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513043 #edb/ref 1074 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- On interval I, $x\\in (-2,-1]\\cup (2,4]$, the function is invertible. Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the restricted graph at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- On interval II, $x\\in [0,2]$, the function is invertible. Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the restricted graph at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- On interval III, $x\\in [-2,2]$, the function is **not** invertible. Some horizontal lines will intersect the restricted graph more than once.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513046 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513033 #edb/ref 175921860454