ible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $[-1,0)$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $0\\le \\frac{1}{2}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $\\frac{1}{2}\\ge \\frac{1}{2}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $(0,1]$, contains the distinct inputs $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\frac{3}{4}$, both with output $\\frac{1}{16}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513209 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513185 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513209 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513186 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513209 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513190 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513209 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513209 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=(x-\\frac{1}{2})^{2},$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in [-1,0)$\n- **II.** $x\\in [- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$\n- **III.** $x\\in (0,1]$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513209 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "808cd4fe-ee5f-5e23-ac63-eaf182540f39" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513209 #edb/ref 1074 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the sam